 Use run_control.py once after Root approval. It launches the reviewed child wrapper, exports the CLOSED report and calls audit_control.py. Export/audit may be retried only as separate read-only analysis if necessary, without repeating GPU execution. Raw reports/full SQLite remain private. Any delivery should use a safe minimal API/NVTX/process/kernel subset and full-original hashes. This is dependency/profiler verification, with no speed or lift conclusion. The two failed large transient attempts remain unqualified, 2.4 remains unbuilt and timing remains prohibited.
 
 V2 changes only warning coverage: the identical unsupported-driver expression scans profile.log, export.log and SQLite DIAGNOSTIC_EVENT. A successful gate records all origins scanned; a rejection includes the originating console line or diagnostic row. V1 remains frozen, and the protocol, probe, launch scope, cases and all binary/library identities are unchanged.
+
+V3 is an AUDIT-ONLY schema correction for the already completed single control. Do not run run_control.py again. Preserve the V2 audit failure and raw trace unchanged. The PROCESSES label must equal the exact approved full executable path, its exact ASCII basename, or (only for a basename longer than15bytes) exactly its first15ASCIIbytes. Arbitrary prefixes and paths with merely matching basenames are rejected. The chosen label mode is explicit and supplementary to unchanged PID/globalPid/NVTX, executable SHA, UID/cwd and numerical gates; start/exit PID birth identity and prelaunch binary/source/core SHA are explicitly reconciled. [Linux proc_pid_comm(5)](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc_pid_comm.5.html) documents TASK_COMM_LEN16 including the terminating null byte. This documents a possible schema label, without claiming Nsight always uses comm. Root may invoke audit_control.py only with a fresh V3-bound audit approval; this causes no application/profile/kernel rerun.
